Correct, no rush. I checked the actual Auth0 SDK cookie name to be sure (`node_modules/@auth0/nextjs-auth0/dist/server/client.js:75` — default is `__session`), and it matches the Caddy config exactly:

```
now.were.cooking {
  route {
    @loggedout_home { path / ; not header Cookie *__session* }
    redir @loggedout_home https://were.cooking
    redir / /today
    ...
```

So in production, Caddy itself already redirects `/` → `/today` whenever the `__session` cookie is present — completely independent of the `page.tsx` code. That edge-level redirect is what was actually masking this for production users the whole time.

The original bug report was specifically about `localhost:3000`, where there's no Caddy in front — just raw `next dev` — so that edge logic doesn't exist there, which is exactly why it was reproducible locally.

So the `hang-auth0-login-redirect` branch fix is still good to merge eventually (fixes local dev, and removes the broken "Open the app" link that sent logged-out users on a pointless round-trip), but it's not fixing anything currently broken in production — no need to rush committing/pushing/deploying it.

